[48.647267, "o", "evt-00039|4624|DC01.corp.example|-|WEC$|3|An account was successfully logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-0-0\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\t-\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\r\r\nLogon Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Type:\t\t3\r\r\n\tRestricted Admin Mode:\t-\r\r\n\tVirtual Account:\t\tNo\r\r\n\tElevated Token:\t\tYes\r\r\n\r\r\nImpersonation Level:\t\tDelegation\r\r\n\r\r\nNew Logon:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-21-2253742117-2054739524-205962475-1111\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tWEC$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP.LOCAL\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x578CA4F\r\r\n\tLinked Logon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Name:\t-\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Domain:\t-\r\r\n\tLogon GUID:\t\t{e9005592-3c39-e94d-f422-b4e34dcd17a4}\r\r\n\r\r\nProcess Information:\r\r\n\tProcess ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tProcess Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\r\r\nNetwork Information:\r\r\n\tWorkstation Name:\t-\r\r\n\tSource Network Address:\t172.18.38.6\r\r\n\tSource Port:\t\t58087\r\r\n\r\r\nDetailed Authentication Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Process:\t\tKerberos\r\r\n\tAuthentication Package:\tKerberos\r\r\n\tTransited Services:\t-\r\r\n\tPackage Name (NTLM only):\t-\r\r\n\tKey Length:\t\t0\r\r\n\r\r\nThis event is generated when a logon session is created. It is generated on the computer that was accessed.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe subject fields indicate the account on the local system which requested the logon. This is most commonly a service such as the Server service, or a local process such as Winlogon.exe or Services.exe.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe logon type field indicates the kind of logon that occurred. The most common types are 2 (interactive) and 3 (network).\r\r\n\r\r\nThe New Logon fields indicate the account for whom the new logon was created, i.e. the account that was logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe network fields indicate where a remote logon request originated. Workstation name is not always available and may be left blank in some cases.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe impersonation level field indicates the extent to which a process in the logon session can impersonate.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe authentication information fields provide detailed information about this specific logon request.\r\r\n\t- Logon GUID is a unique identifier that can be used to correlate this event with a KDC event.\r\r\n\t- Transited services indicate which intermediate services have part\r\n"]
[48.647532, "o", "evt-00063|4624|DC01.corp.example|-|WEC$|3|An account was successfully logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-0-0\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\t-\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\r\r\nLogon Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Type:\t\t3\r\r\n\tRestricted Admin Mode:\t-\r\r\n\tVirtual Account:\t\tNo\r\r\n\tElevated Token:\t\tYes\r\r\n\r\r\nImpersonation Level:\t\tImpersonation\r\r\n\r\r\nNew Logon:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-21-2253742117-2054739524-205962475-1111\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tWEC$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP.LOCAL\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x578CB44\r\r\n\tLinked Logon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Name:\t-\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Domain:\t-\r\r\n\tLogon GUID:\t\t{6a899b80-ff65-11d2-fc15-383cea43e4fe}\r\r\n\r\r\nProcess Information:\r\r\n\tProcess ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tProcess Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\r\r\nNetwork Information:\r\r\n\tWorkstation Name:\t-\r\r\n\tSource Network Address:\t172.18.38.6\r\r\n\tSource Port:\t\t58089\r\r\n\r\r\nDetailed Authentication Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Process:\t\tKerberos\r\r\n\tAuthentication Package:\tKerberos\r\r\n\tTransited Services:\t-\r\r\n\tPackage Name (NTLM only):\t-\r\r\n\tKey Length:\t\t0\r\r\n\r\r\nThis event is generated when a logon session is created. It is generated on the computer that was accessed.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe subject fields indicate the account on the local system which requested the logon. This is most commonly a service such as the Server service, or a local process such as Winlogon.exe or Services.exe.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe logon type field indicates the kind of logon that occurred. The most common types are 2 (interactive) and 3 (network).\r\r\n\r\r\nThe New Logon fields indicate the account for whom the new logon was created, i.e. the account that was logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe network fields indicate where a remote logon request originated. Workstation name is not always available and may be left blank in some cases.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe impersonation level field indicates the extent to which a process in the logon session can impersonate.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe authentication information fields provide detailed information about this specific logon request.\r\r\n\t- Logon GUID is a unique identifier that can be used to correlate this event with a KDC event.\r\r\n\t- Transited services indicate which intermediate services have p\r\nevt-00067|4624|DC01.corp.example|-|WEC$|3|An account was successfully logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-0-0\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\t-\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\r\r\nLogon Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Type:\t\t3\r\r\n\tRestricted Admin Mode:\t-\r\r\n\tVirtual Account:\t\tNo\r\r\n\tElevated Token:\t\tYes\r\r\n\r\r\nImpersonation Level:\t\tImpersonation\r\r\n\r\r\nNew Logon:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-21-2253742117-2054739524-205962475-1111\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tWEC$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP.LOCAL\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x578CB5D\r\r\n\tLinked Logon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Name:\t-\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Domain:\t-\r\r\n\tLogon GUID:\t\t{6a899b80-ff65-11d2-fc15-383cea43e4fe}\r\r\n\r\r\nProcess Information:\r\r\n\tProcess ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tProcess Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\r\r\nNetwork Information:\r\r\n\tWorkstation Name:\t-\r\r\n\tSource Network Address:\t172.18.38.6\r\r\n\tSource Port:\t\t58090\r\r\n\r\r\nDetailed Authentication Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Process:\t\tKerberos\r\r\n\tAuthentication Package:\tKerberos\r\r\n\tTransited Services:\t-\r\r\n\tPackage Name (NTLM only):\t-\r\r\n\tKey Length:\t\t0\r\r\n\r\r\nThis event is generated when a logon session is created. It is generated on the computer that was accessed.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe subject fields indicate the account on the local system which requested the logon. This is most commonly a service such as the Server service, or a local process such as Winlogon.exe or Services.exe.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe logon type field indicates the kind of logon that occurred. The most common types are 2 (interactive) and 3 (network).\r\r\n\r\r\nThe New Logon fields indicate the account for whom the new logon was created, i.e. the account that was logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe network fields indicate where a remote logon request originated. Workstation name is not always available and may be left blank in some cases.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe impersonation level field indicates the extent to which a process in the logon session can impersonate.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe authentication information fields provide detailed information about this specific logon request.\r\r\n\t- Logon GUID is a unique identifier that can be used to correla"]
[48.647572, "o", "te this event with a KDC event.\r\r\n\t- Transited services indicate which intermediate services have p\r\nevt-00272|4703|DC01.corp.example|DC01$|DC01$||A token right was adjusted.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-18\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tDC01$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x3E7\r\r\n\r\r\nTarget Account:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-0-0\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tDC01$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x3E7\r\r\n\r\r\nProcess Information:\r\r\n\tProcess ID:\t\t0x934\r\r\n\tProcess Name:\t\tC:\\Windows\\System32\\svchost.exe\r\r\n\r\r\nEnabled Privileges:\r\r\n\t\t\tSeAssignPrimaryTokenPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeIncreaseQuotaPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeTakeOwnershipPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeLoadDriverPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeSystemtimePrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeBackupPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeRestorePrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeShutdownPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeSystemEnvironmentPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeUndockPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeManageVolumePrivilege\r\r\n\r\r\nDisabled Privileges:\r\r\n\t\t\t-\r\n"]
[48.647866, "o", "evt-00549|4672|DC01.corp.example|DC01$|||Special privileges assigned to new logon.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-18\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tDC01$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x578EED5\r\r\n\r\r\nPrivileges:\t\tSeSecurityPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeBackupPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeRestorePrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeTakeOwnershipPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeDebugPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeSystemEnvironmentPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeLoadDriverPrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeImpersonatePrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeDelegateSessionUserImpersonatePrivilege\r\r\n\t\t\tSeEnableDelegationPrivilege\r\nevt-00550|4624|DC01.corp.example|-|DC01$|3|An account was successfully logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-0-0\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\t-\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\r\r\nLogon Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Type:\t\t3\r\r\n\tRestricted Admin Mode:\t-\r\r\n\tVirtual Account:\t\tNo\r\r\n\tElevated Token:\t\tYes\r\r\n\r\r\nImpersonation Level:\t\tImpersonation\r\r\n\r\r\nNew Logon:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-18\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tDC01$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP.LOCAL\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x578EED5\r\r\n\tLinked Logon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Name:\t-\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Domain:\t-\r\r\n\tLogon GUID:\t\t{25156ef9-7f56-4162-9dc2-e9b934b8f1af}\r\r\n\r\r\nProcess Information:\r\r\n\tProcess ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tProcess Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\r\r\nNetwork Information:\r\r\n\tWorkstation Name:\t-\r\r\n\tSource Network Address:\t::1\r\r\n\tSource Port:\t\t64885\r\r\n\r\r\nDetailed Authentication Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Process:\t\tKerberos\r\r\n\tAuthentication Package:\tKerberos\r\r\n\tTransited Services:\t-\r\r\n\tPackage Name (NTLM only):\t-\r\r\n\tKey Length:\t\t0\r\r\n\r\r\nThis event is generated when a logon session is created. It is generated on the computer that was accessed.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe subject fields indicate the account on the local system which requested the logon. This is most commonly a service such as the Server service, or a local process such as Winlogon.exe or Services.exe.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe logon type field indicates the kind of logon that occurred. The most common types are 2 (interactive) and 3 (network).\r\r\n\r\r\nThe New Logon fields indicate the account for whom the new logon was created, i.e. the account that was logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe network fields indicate where a remote logon request originated. Workstation name is not always available and may be left blank in some cases.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe impersonation level field indicates the extent to which a process in the logon session can impersonate.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe authentication information fields provide detailed information about this specific logon request.\r\r\n\t- Logon GUID is a unique identifier that can be used to correlate this event with a KDC event.\r\r\n\t- Transited services indicate which intermediate services have participated in this logon request.\r\r\n\t- P\r\n"]
[48.648483, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@d9cad5995bec:~# "]
[53.228477, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/logons.py\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\"/root/data/eventlogs.db\")\nconn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\ncurr = conn.cursor()\n\ncurr.execute(\"SELECT event_id, host, timestamp, message FROM events WHERE event_code = 4624\")\nfor row in curr:\n    msg = row['message']\n    # extract Account Name, Logon Type, Source Network Address\n    accounts = re.findall(r'Account Name:\\s+(\\S+)', msg)\n    logon_type = re.findall(r'Logon Type:\\s+(\\d+)', msg)\n    source_ip = re.findall(r'Source Network Address:\\s+(\\S+)', msg)\n    # usually there are multiple Account Names, the second one is the target user\n    target_user = accounts[1] if len(accounts) > 1 else (accounts[0] if accounts else \"Unknown\")\n    ltype = logon_type[0] if logon_type else \"Unknown\"\n    sip = source_ip[0] if source_ip else \"Unknown\"\n    print(f\"ID: {row['event_id']} | Host: {row['host']} | Time: {row['timestamp']} | Target User: {target_user} | Logon Type: {ltype} | Src IP: {sip}\")\nEOF\npython3 /tmp/logons.py\n"]
[53.228716, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/logons.py\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
